body:
The user-level `default-template-library` setting: when set, its value is the directory of the template library that `kalide init` uses when no path argument is given. The value is a directory path; on `set` it is normalised to an absolute path — a leading `~` expands to the user's home directory and a relative path resolves against the process's current working directory — so the stored value is absolute and independent of the directory any later command runs in. The default library is used exactly as an explicit `kalide init <path>` library is: the created deck references it (`templates: <path>`), has no local `templates/` and no starter slide (external-template-library). The library at the stored path is validated fail-fast exactly as an explicit path is (cli-init): a missing or invalid library, or a library that does not resolve the deck's theme, aborts init with a clear error naming the path, a non-zero exit, and nothing written. The setting is read from the per-user config file (cli-config) by every command that consumes it, and kalide never reaches the network to resolve it. When the setting is absent, init falls back to the built-in starter deck (init-library-resolution).

AC:
- after `kalide config set default-template-library ../shared-lib` (and a `get` showing the resolved absolute path), `kalide init` in an empty directory writes a deck whose `kalide.yaml` has `templates: <absolute path>`, an empty `slides/`, an empty `assets/`, no local `templates/` and no starter slide, and `kalide start` validates and serves it (the deck's theme resolves);
- with the default set to a path that is missing or not a valid library, `kalide init` fails with a non-zero exit naming the path and writes nothing;
- the stored value is absolute regardless of the working directory of the setting command.
